[[File:Iss016e019375.jpg|thumb|The southern terminus of the Suez Canal at [[Suez]] on the [[Gulf of Suez]], at the northern end of the [[Red Sea]]]]
[[File:Aerial view of city of Suez and Suez Canal.jpg|thumb|Aerial view of the Suez Canal at Suez]]

The '''Suez Canal''' ({{lang-ar|قَنَاةُ ٱلسُّوَيْسِ}}, ''{{transl|arz|ALA|Qanātu as-Suways}}'') is an artificial [[sea-level]] [[waterway]] in [[Egypt]], connecting the [[Mediterranean Sea]] to the [[Red Sea]] through the [[Isthmus of Suez]] and dividing Africa and Asia. The canal is part of the [[Silk Road]] that connects Europe with Asia.

In 1858, [[Ferdinand de Lesseps]] formed the [[Suez Canal Company]] for the express purpose of building the [[canal]]. Construction of the canal lasted from 1859 to 1869. The canal officially opened on 17 November 1869. It offers vessels a direct route between the [[Atlantic Ocean#NORTH ATLANTIC|North Atlantic]] and northern [[Indian Ocean|Indian]] oceans via the Mediterranean Sea and the Red Sea, avoiding the South Atlantic and southern Indian oceans and reducing the journey distance from the Arabian Sea to London by approximately {{convert|8900|km||abbr=}}, or 10 days at {{convert|20|kn}} to 8 days at {{convert|24|kn}}.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.worldshipping.org/pdf/suez-canal-presentation.pdf |title=The Suez Canal – A vital shortcut for global commerce |publisher=[[World Shipping Council]] |access-date=15 March 2019 |archive-date=22 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180422172437/http://www.worldshipping.org/pdf/suez-canal-presentation.pdf |url-status=live}}</ref> The canal extends from the northern terminus of [[Port Said]] to the southern terminus of [[Port Tewfik]] at the city of [[Suez]]. Its length is {{convert|193.30|km|mi|abbr=on}} including its northern and southern access-channels. In 2020, more than 18,500 vessels traversed the canal (an average of 51.5 per day).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Yeung |first1=Jessie |title=Suez Canal authorities need to remove up to 706,000 cubic feet of sand to free the ''Ever Given'' |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/25/middleeast/suez-canal-ship-sand-intl-hnk/index.html |access-date=26 March 2021 |publisher=CNN |date=March 26, 2021 |archive-date=26 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210326183954/https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/25/middleeast/suez-canal-ship-sand-intl-hnk/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref>{{update after|2021}}

The original canal featured a single-lane waterway with passing locations in the Ballah Bypass and the [[Great Bitter Lake]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.suezcanal.gov.eg/ |title=Suez Canal Authority |access-date=2 May 2010 |archive-date=13 June 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140613040705/http://www.suezcanal.gov.eg/ |url-status=live }}</ref> It contained, according to [[Alois Negrelli]]'s plans, no [[Lock (water transport)|lock]] systems, with seawater flowing freely through it. In general, the water in the canal north of the Bitter Lakes flows north in winter and south in summer. South of the lakes, the current changes with the [[tide]] at Suez.<ref name=rsp>{{cite book |title=The Red Sea Pilot |publisher=Imray Laurie Norie & Wilson |year=1995 |author1=Elaine Morgan |author2=Stephen Davies |isbn=9780852885543 |page=266}}</ref>

While the canal was the property of the Egyptian government, European shareholders, mostly British and French, owned the [[Suez Canal Company|concessionary company]] which operated it until July 1956, when President [[Gamal Abdel Nasser]] nationalized it—an event which led to the [[Suez Crisis]] of October–November 1956.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2009-11-09 |url=https://www.history.com/topics/cold-war/suez-crisis |title=Suez Crisis |department=History.com |publisher=A&E Television Networks |access-date=2019-04-17 |archive-date=30 March 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190330061318/https://www.

